  "markscheme": {
    "html": "<p>B</p>\n<p>The principle of conservation of charge requires that for a steady current in a single, unbranched conductor, the current must be the same at all points. Therefore, the current in section X is equal to the current in section Y (<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I_{X}\"></span> = <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I_{Y}\"></span>). The ratio <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I_{Y}\"></span> / <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I_{X}\"></span> is thus 1. The different radii affect the current density and electron drift speed, but not the total current.</p>"
  },
  "paper": "paper_1a",
  "question": {
    "html": "<p>A current-carrying wire has two sections with different radii.</p>\n<p>The radius of the wire in section X is twice that in section Y. The current in X is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I_{X}\"></span>, and the current in Y is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I_{Y}\"></span>. What is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I_{Y}\"></span> / <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I_{X}\"></span>?</p>\n<figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure>\n<ol class=\"mcq-options\">\n<li data-label=\"A\"> <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"1/2\"></span></li>\n<li data-label=\"B\"> <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"1\"></span></li>\n<li data-label=\"C\"> <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"2\"></span></li>\n<li data-label=\"D\"> <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"4\"></span></li>\n</ol>"
  },
